      "definition": "Meditative concentration (Sanskrit dhyāna, Old Bengali jhāṇa; Tibetan bsam gtan): absorption of the mind in a single object, one of the six perfections and the name of four levels of meditation. Song 34 (3a) asks what use it is, with its exposition; Munidatta has 'meditation and explanation' (dhyānavyākhyāna) (cp.34.m4). Distinct from bhāvanā, which the glossary renders 'meditation'.",
      "symbolicImage": "",
      "symbolicReadings": [],
      "forbiddenInLine": [],
      "rationale": "Dhyāna (jhāṇa, bsam gtan) is a different term from bhāvanā (sgom pa), which already holds \"meditation\"; \"concentration\" keeps the two apart, with \"meditation\" as a variant for the line."
