      "definition": "Self and other (Sanskrit sva, para; Tibetan rang gzhan): the division into one's own and another's, which siddha teaching says the realized no longer make. In 4b Dārika does not see svaparāpara, 'self, other and the rest' (apara may be 'not other', or parāpara 'far and near'); Munidatta: he sees no division of self and other in saṃsāra (cp.34.m5).",
      "symbolicImage": "",
      "symbolicReadings": [],
      "forbiddenInLine": [],
      "rationale": "The nonduality of self and other is the point of 4b and a recurring siddha theme; the third member of the compound is unclear, so the entry takes the clear pair."
    },
