        }
      ],
      "definition": "A king (Old Bengali rāā, rāa; Tibetan rgyal po). In song 34 (5a) the poet cries 'king, king, king', and says other kings are bound by delusion. Munidatta takes the three cries as pointing to his own qualities, lordship of the body and the rest (cp.34.m6). Not ṭhākura, the 'king' of Kāṇha's game, nor the king of mind.",
      "symbolicImage": "'king, king, king', and the other kings bound by delusion",
      "symbolicReadings": [
        {
          "referent": "the poet's own qualities, beginning with lordship of the body (kāyaiśvarya), which the three utterances point to; the Tibetan has 'his own activity (bya ba), lordship and other qualities', perhaps reading kārya for kāya",
          "per": "Munidatta",
          "where": [
            "cp.34.m6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "referent": "the other kings: gods, lords of nāgas and the like, who stay bound by delusion about sense objects, while the poet, by Lūyīpāda's grace, has the twelve stages and is equal to the Jinas; the Tibetan agrees (lha dang klu'i dbang po la sogs pa yul la rmongs pas bcings)",
          "per": "Munidatta",
          "where": [
            "cp.34.m6"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "forbiddenInLine": [
        "lordship",
        "lordship of the body",
        "qualities",
        "nāga lords",
        "lords of nāgas"
      ],
      "rationale": "The thrice-cried king of 5a is an image Munidatta reads; ṭhākura, already rendered \"king\", is a different word (the game piece), so rājā needs its own entry."
    },
